Run **stateless Workers** at 300+ PoPs, store blobs in **R2** (S3-compatible, zero egress), cache at the edge with `cache.put` + Tiered Cache, and use **D1/KV/Durable Objects** for state — origin is R2, not a VM.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">User -> nearest Worker PoP -> R2 (origin) -> edge cache + D1/KV</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Workers</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">V8 isolates</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">0ms cold start</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">per-request billing</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">R2 Storage</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">S3 API, no egress fee</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">multi-region auto</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">presigned URLs</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Edge State</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">KV (eventual)</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">D1 (SQLite edge)</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Durable Objects (strong)</text></svg>

**Architecture.** **Workers** run your API/SSR at the edge (V8 isolates, not containers — sub-10ms cold start). Static assets and uploads go to **R2**; Workers read/write via S3 API and generate **presigned URLs** for direct browser upload. Use **Cache API** (`caches.default`) and **Cache Rules** for `stale-while-revalidate` on GETs; R2 + `Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400` gives global CDN without origin. For state: **KV** for config/feature flags (eventually consistent), **D1** for relational queries at edge, **Durable Objects** for per-entity strong consistency (rooms, counters). Add **Queues** for async work and **R2 event notifications** to trigger Workers on upload.

| Layer | Use | Avoid |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Workers | auth, SSR, API | heavy compute >30s, native binaries |
| R2 | blobs, uploads, origin | small hot keys (use KV/cache) |
| Durable Objects | WebSocket coordinator, locks | global tables (use D1) |
| KV | flags, sessions | strongly consistent reads |

> **Interview tip:** Mention **zero egress fees** and why R2 changes cost modeling vs S3, and that Workers have **no filesystem** — that distinction often decides the design.

```typescript
export default {
  async fetch(req, env) {
    const url = new URL(req.url);
    // serve from edge cache first
    let res = await caches.default.match(req);
    if (res) return res;
    const obj = await env.MY_BUCKET.get(url.pathname.slice(1));
    if (!obj) return new Response('Not found', {status:404});
    res = new Response(obj.body, {
      headers: {'Content-Type': obj.httpMetadata.contentType, 'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=3600'}
    });
    // tiered cache
    await caches.default.put(req, res.clone());
    return res;
  }
}
```

Combine **Tiered Cache + Cache-Tag purging + SWR**: cache `public, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=600` at edge, tag with `cacheTags`, and purge by tag on mutation — hit rate high, staleness bounded.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Origin -> Tiered Cache (upper) -> PoP cache -> SWR serve-stale -> purge by tag</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Cache-Control</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">s-maxage 60</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">SWR 600s</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">stale ok, fast</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Cache Tags</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">tag: product:123</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">purge by tag</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">instant global</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Tiered Cache</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Argo Smart Tier</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">1 upper fetch</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">origin shield</text></svg>

**How it works.** Set **`CDN-Cache-Control: s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=600`** — edge serves stale while revalidating in background, so no request waits on origin. Add **`Cache-Tag: product:123, category:shoes`** on responses; on write, call `purgeCacheByTags(['product:123'])` for **instant global invalidation** (vs URL purge). Enable **Tiered Cache** so only one upper-tier PoP fetches origin. For personalized content, use `Vary` or `cacheEverything: false` + `bypassCache` cookie; for R2 objects, set `Cache-Control` on PUT and use **R2 Cache Reserve**.

| Strategy | Hit rate | Staleness |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TTL only | low if short | bounded |
| SWR + tags | 95%+ | max s-maxage (60s) |
| No cache | 0% | 0 |
| Tiered + SWR | 98% | same, fewer origins |

> **Interview tip:** Say **stale-while-revalidate + purge-by-tag** is the 2025 standard — it avoids the classic TTL vs freshness dilemma and impresses edge-savvy interviewers.

```typescript
// Worker: cache with tags + SWR
async function cachedFetch(req, env) {
  const cached = await caches.default.match(req);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const origin = await fetch(req);
  const res = new Response(origin.body, origin);
  res.headers.set('CDN-Cache-Control', 'public, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=600');
  res.headers.set('Cache-Tag', 'product:123, category:shoes');
  await caches.default.put(req, res.clone());
  return res;
}
// on mutation
await fetch('https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/zones/xxx/purge_cache',
  {method:'POST', body: JSON.stringify({tags:['product:123']})})
```

Instrument with **OTel SDKs**, propagate **W3C TraceContext**, collect via **OTel Collector** (tail sampling), and store in **Tempo/Jaeger + ClickHouse** with **exemplars** linking metrics->traces.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">SDK (trace) -> Collector (tail sample) -> Tempo/Jaeger -> Grafana + exemplars</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Instrumentation</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">auto + manual spans</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">W3C header</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">baggage context</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Collector</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">tail sampling</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">filter PII</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">batch + retry</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Backend</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">Tempo/Jaeger</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ClickHouse logs</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">exemplar link</text></svg>

**Architecture.** Each service uses **OTel SDK** (auto-instrumentation for HTTP/gRPC/DB + manual `tracer.startSpan` for domain ops). **Context propagation** via `traceparent: 00-traceId-parentId-flags` across REST/gRPC/Kafka headers. **Collectors** run as sidecars + gateway: **head sampling** (1% at SDK) for cost, **tail sampling** (sample errors + high-latency + 1% of success) at gateway for useful traces. Export OTLP to **Tempo/Grafana** or **Jaeger** with **ClickHouse** for storage. Correlate **logs** by injecting `trace_id` into every log line (trace-to-logs), and add **exemplars** to Prometheus histograms so a latency spike links to a trace. Alert on **SLO burn** via traces (p95 > threshold).

| Choice | Pros | Cons |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Head sampling | cheap | drops interesting traces |
| Tail sampling | keeps errors/latency | needs collector memory |
| No sampling | complete | cost/storage explosion |
| Collector gateway | single config | extra hop |

> **Interview tip:** Mention **tail sampling keeps 100% of errors** and that you **inject trace_id into logs** — that one line answers the classic 'how do you find logs for a trace?' follow-up.

```typescript
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
const tracer = trace.getTracer('checkout');
app.post('/order', async (req,res)=>{
  return tracer.startActiveSpan('checkout.handle', async span=>{
    try {
      span.setAttribute('order.id', req.body.id);
      await chargePayment(req.body); // child spans auto-linked
      span.setStatus({code:1});
      res.json({ok:true});
    } catch(e){ span.recordException(e); throw e; }
    finally { span.end(); }
  });
});
```

Make **WS servers stateless** behind L4 LB, store **user->server** in Redis, fan-out via **Redis Pub/Sub or Streams** across servers — any server can publish, the owning server pushes to the socket.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Client WS -> L4 LB -> WS pod (holds socket) -> Redis pub/sub -> all pods -> push to owner</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">WS Pods</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">stateful sockets</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">holds fd</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">scale by conns</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Redis Pub/Sub</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">fan-out bus</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ephemeral</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">at-most-once</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Redis Streams</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">persisted</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">replay + consumer</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">at-least-once</text></svg>

**Architecture.** Clients connect via **L4 (NLB)** to any **WS pod** (Node/Go `ws`); pod stores `user:123 -> pod-7` in **Redis** with TTL + heartbeat. To send a message to user 123, any service does `PUBLISH user:123 {msg}`; every pod is subscribed, the pod that owns the socket delivers. For **rooms**, `PUBLISH room:abc`. Use **Redis Cluster pub/sub** or **Streams** if you need persistence/replay; plain pub/sub drops if subscriber down. Handle **reconnect** with **resume token + missed stream ID**, **backpressure** via per-socket queue (drop oldest or `1013 Try Again Later`), and **presence** via `SETEX presence:123 30` + gossip. Scale pods on `active_conns` (not CPU); use **consistent hashing** for room affinity if needed.

| Concern | Solution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fan-out | Redis pub/sub |
| Persistence | Redis Streams / Kafka |
| Presence | Redis TTL + heartbeat |
| Resume | stream ID / sequence |
| Backpressure | bounded queue + drop/close |

> **Interview tip:** Mention **pub/sub is fire-and-forget** — if you need delivery during deploy, use **Streams** with consumer groups; that nuance separates junior from senior.

```typescript
import Redis from 'ioredis';
const pub = new Redis(), sub = new Redis();
sub.subscribe('room:123');
sub.on('message', (_, msg)=>{
  for(const sock of roomSockets.get('room:123')||[]) sock.send(msg);
});
// any service publishes
await pub.publish('room:123', JSON.stringify({text:'hi'}));
// presence
await pub.setex('presence:'+userId, 30, podId);
```

Avoid **sticky sessions** — store **presence in Redis** and route via **pub/sub**; sticky breaks rolling deploys and balance. Use **sequence IDs + Redis Stream** for lossless resume.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Stateless WS (any pod) -> Redis presence -> Stream backlog -> resume by last_id</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Sticky LB</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">IP hash</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">hot spots</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">deploy pain</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Stateless+Redis</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">any pod</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">pub/sub fan-out</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">even balance</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Resume</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">last_id + Stream</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">replay missed</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">no loss</text></svg>

**How it works.** **Sticky (IP hash)** ties a user to a pod — simple but causes **hot spots** and **draining pain** on deploy (clients must reconnect). **Stateless** lets any pod accept any user; presence (`presence:{user}` -> `podId` with `EX 30`) is the source of truth. On disconnect, client reconnects anywhere and asks `RESUME last_id=456`; server does `XREAD STREAM room:123 FROM 456` to replay. Use **Durable Objects** or **Redis Streams** for room state if you need strong consistency (e.g., cursor position). Monitor `ws_active_connections` and recycle pods gracefully with `SIGTERM + 30s drain`.

| Approach | Load balance | Deploy | Resume |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sticky | skewed | draining issues | no replay |
| Stateless+pubsub | even | any pod dies safely | needs Streams |
| Stateless+Streams | even | safe | lossless |

> **Interview tip:** Say **sticky is an anti-pattern for WebSockets in 2025** — scale via stateless + Redis, mention **draining** and **resume ID** to ace the follow-up.

```typescript
// resume with Redis Streams
const lastId = ws.lastSeenId || '0-0';
const missed = await redis.xrange('room:123', lastId, '+', 'COUNT', 100);
for(const [,fields] of missed) ws.send(fields[1]);
// heartbeat
setInterval(()=> redis.expire('presence:'+userId, 30), 10000);
```

Split file into **5 MB-5 GB parts** (1k-10k), upload parts in **parallel via presigned URLs**, then **CompleteMultipartUpload** with part ETags — resume by listing uploaded parts.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">CreateMultipart -> presign part URLs -> parallel PUT 5MB+ -> Complete (ETags)</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Create</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">returns uploadId</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">presign per part</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">parallel 10x</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Upload parts</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">PUT ?partNumber & uploadId</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ETag per part</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">retry per part</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Complete</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">POST with ETags</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">atomic object</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">abort on fail</text></svg>

**How it works.** Client calls backend `CreateMultipartUpload` -> gets `uploadId` + **presigned PUT URLs** per part (no proxy through backend). Upload **5-10 MB parts** in parallel (6-10 concurrency saturates bandwidth); each returns an **ETag**. On failure, `ListParts` shows what's done — **resume** only missing parts. On success, `CompleteMultipartUpload` sends `[{PartNumber, ETag}]` and S3 assembles atomically; verify with **SHA256 checksums** (`ChecksumSHA256` per part + object). Set **lifecycle** `AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload days=7` to avoid orphan charges. For direct browser UX, also support **S3 Transfer Acceleration** or **R2 multipart**. Max 10k parts, so choose part size = `ceil(file/10000)`.

| Concern | Solution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Parallelism | presigned per part, 6-10 conc |
| Resume | ListParts |
| Integrity | ETag + ChecksumSHA256 |
| Orphan cost | abort lifecycle 7d |
| 5TB limit | part size = file/10000 |

> **Interview tip:** Mention **presigned URLs keep backend out of data path** and **abort lifecycle** — both are cost/ops gotchas interviewers hunt for.

```typescript
// backend: create + presign
const {UploadId} = await s3.createMultipartUpload({Bucket, Key}).promise();
const urls = await Promise.all(Array.from({length: parts}, (_,i)=>
  s3.getSignedUrlPromise('uploadPart', {Bucket, Key, UploadId, PartNumber: i+1, Expires: 3600})));
// client: upload in parallel, then complete
const etags = await Promise.all(urls.map((u,i)=> fetch(u,{method:'PUT', body: chunks[i]}).then(r=>({ETag: r.headers.get('ETag'), PartNumber: i+1}))));
await s3.completeMultipartUpload({Bucket, Key, UploadId, MultipartUpload:{Parts: etags}}).promise();
```

Use **presigned multipart + `ListParts` resume + `ChecksumSHA256`** per part; browser computes chunk hashes, S3 validates, and `Complete` assembles only if checksums match — no corrupt object.

<svg class="iq-diagram" viewBox="0 0 820 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="18" font-size="11">Chunk + hash -> PUT presigned (Checksum) -> ListParts on retry -> Complete with checksums</text><rect x="40" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#22c55e" stroke="#22c55e"/><text x="150" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#22c55e">Chunk</text><text x="150" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">5-10 MB</text><text x="150" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">hash SHA256</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">per part</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b"/><text x="410" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#f59e0b">Resume</text><text x="410" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ListParts</text><text x="410" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">skip done</text><text x="410" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">idempotent retry</text><rect x="560" y="40" width="220" height="90" rx="14" class="d-box" fill="#3b82f6" stroke="#3b82f6"/><text x="670" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#3b82f6">Verify</text><text x="670" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">ChecksumSHA256</text><text x="670" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">S3 400 on mismatch</text><text x="670" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">end-to-end</text></svg>

**How it works.** Browser slices `file.slice(n*SIZE, (n+1)*SIZE)`, computes `SHA256` per chunk (SubtleCrypto), sends hashes to backend which creates multipart with `ChecksumType: SHA256` and returns presigned URLs + `uploadId`. Each `PUT ?partNumber` includes `ChecksumSHA256: <base64>`; S3 rejects on mismatch. On network drop, client calls `GET /upload/{id}/parts` (backend `ListParts`) to learn ETags done and re-upload only gaps. Add **progress** via `onUploadProgress` per chunk and **concurrency cap** (3-6). For small files (<100 MB) fallback to **single presigned PUT**; for huge, increase part size to stay under 10k part limit.

| Step | Header/Call | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Create | `ChecksumType: SHA256` | enable checksum |
| Upload | `ChecksumSHA256: ...` | per-part integrity |
| List | `ListParts` | resume |
| Complete | `ChecksumSHA256` agg | object integrity |

> **Interview tip:** Say you **always send per-part checksums** — S3's 2024 checksum enforcement catches bit flips that ETag alone misses; it is the 2025 best practice.

```typescript
